WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.010
- Well, welcome, everybody,

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:08.373
to the public board
meeting of NHS England.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.290
Apologies for absence.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.710
We've got Julian Kelly is away.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.210
Munir Pirmohamed is also away.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.070
I hope this may be our
last meeting virtually.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.663
I hope next, maybe in March,

00:00:22.663 --> 00:00:24.300
we'll all be able to meet together again,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.240
but in the meantime,

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.100
I'm afraid we had to exclude
members of the public

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.503
from coming here for a physical meeting.

00:00:33.025 --> 00:00:34.570
Are there any declarations of interests

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:35.900
that are not already declared

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.700
anyone would like to raise?

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:38.533
No, thank you.

00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:39.740
Can I approve the minutes

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.693
of the meeting held on
the 27th of November?

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:44.970
Good, thank you.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:49.170
Well, we got a fairly short
meeting today because obviously,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.720
the executive team have
been very heavily engaged

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.130
in dealing with the Omicron pandemic

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.430
over the last couple of months,

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.760
but I hope that the next meeting

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.440
we'll have some substantive
strategic issues on the agenda,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.873
but, Amanda, over to you for
your report, please, thank you.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.950
- Thank you, David.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.500
Needless to say,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.010
it has been an extremely
pressured couple of months

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.560
since we last met.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.410
The NHS declared a
level-four national incident

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:21.860
on the 13th of December

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.420
in response to the new challenges

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.190
posed by the Omicron variant.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.070
In the face of that pressure

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.430
and thanks, I have to say yet again,

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.080
to an incredible response

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.260
from NHS colleagues, our partners,

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.170
and, of course, volunteers,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.840
we have achieved some real successes.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.830
The COVID-19 vaccination programme
was significantly sped up

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.870
to provide the maximum
possible level of protection

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:46.703
to the public

00:01:46.703 --> 00:01:49.890
ahead of the anticipated
peak in infections.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.180
Indeed, between the incident declaration

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.420
and the end of the year,

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.860
almost nine million additional
booster and third doses

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.340
were administered, and now,

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.530
more than four in five eligible adults

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.860
have received a third or booster dose.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:08.840
It's just worth noting
that over 550,000 of those

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.400
were co-administered with flu.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.670
That is a real positive,

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:14.950
given the logistical
challenges of doing that,

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.380
the COVID vaccine and
the flu vaccine together,

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:18.800
but it really does show the commitment

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.720
to making every contact count.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.360
We also began the initial phases

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.970
of rolling out the COVID-19 new treatments

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:28.550
so monoclonal antibodies and antivirals

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.490
for those in the highest risk groups.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.560
That was through new
COVID-medicines-delivery units.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.160
Now more than 10,000 people have benefited

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.100
from these treatments.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.820
We did a huge amount of
work to create capacity

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.560
ready for a potential surge

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.690
in patients needing hospital care.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.140
Now, that was both in
terms of providing support

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.010
to leave hospital

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:50.830
but also creating additional capacity

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:53.580
through things like the
Nightingale surge hubs

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:54.413
and the arrangement

00:02:54.413 --> 00:02:56.160
the Secretary of State directed us to make

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.550
with independent hospital chains.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.300
Discharge, as you know,
has been a major focus.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.230
We've provided support

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:04.270
through a range of actions,

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.260
including the national contract

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.940
with Hospice UK

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.380
to support up to 4,800 people per day

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.580
in hospice beds or their own homes,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.010
funding to pay for the
use of beds in hotels,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.000
care homes, and increase use

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.920
of the virtual ward model
and COVID oximetry at home.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.780
We did see a really strong start

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.020
with more than 56,000 people supported

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.440
to go home for Christmas

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.490
over the five days immediately beforehand,

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:30.830
but, of course, we do all know

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.820
that making the sustained
headway we all wanted to see

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.360
has been really hard,

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:36.900
partly due, of course,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.560
to the significant capacity constraints

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.100
caused by some of the impact on staff

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.950
as a consequence of COVID

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:48.060
and some of those capacity
constraints also in social care,

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.150
with lots of regions reporting

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.110
most or indeed a significant proportion

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.980
of care homes and
domiciliary care providers

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:56.820
not being able to take on new people

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.190
over that period.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.680
Staffing issues, as I
said, were obviously seen

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.270
across the NHS

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.780
with Omicron being responsible

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.430
for more than half of
all absences in trusts

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:08.720
in early January,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.070
which is well over a
million days lost in total

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:13.520
just in secondary care.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:14.353
We know, of course,

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:18.220
that primary care teams have
faced similar issues too.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.690
We've talked before about demand

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.680
for urgent and emergency care

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:23.010
and the impact of constraints

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.790
on capacity and on patient
flow through hospitals.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.840
We have seen real pressure,

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.050
particularly on ambulance services,

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.480
on response times, in particular,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.510
and on A and E departments.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.770
That is a continued national focus.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.350
We're supporting the NHS to improve

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.170
with some encouraging signs, actually,

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.430
in the last couple of weeks.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.770
We may want to talk more about that,

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.810
but in spite of that
very significant pressure

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.380
and thanks to the measures put in place

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.940
since previous winter wave,

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.600
early indications suggest

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.840
we have seen elective and
diagnostic performance maintained

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.170
at higher levels than last year

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.200
through December and into January.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.440
That builds on the
positive position we saw

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.770
in the published statistics for November,

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.187
which showed elective activity 13% up

00:05:10.187 --> 00:05:15.140
and elective procedures
up 8% on November 2020.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.820
We've also continued,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:18.464
despite all of that to work,

00:05:18.464 --> 00:05:21.270
to make the best and
latest treatments available

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:22.810
to NHS patients,

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.060
over the past two months agreeing deals

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.730
to provide sapropterin to all PKU patients

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:29.630
for the first time,

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.300
Palforzia, which is a pioneer in treatment

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.720
for a peanut allergy

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.533
that will benefit thousands
of children and young people.

00:05:36.533 --> 00:05:39.560
That is on top of over 1,000 more children

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.230
living with cystic fibrosis

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:43.550
being able to access Kaftrio

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.840
thanks to our existing portfolio deal

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.880
after MRHA extended the licence.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:52.270
Look, once again, I just want
to put on record my thanks

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.530
to every single member of the NHS team

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.050
who has worked so hard
and with such dedication

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:03.170
to care for patients and pull
out all the stops once again

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.320
to do so

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.550
and particularly for those for whom

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:09.160
doing so meant sacrificing
time with their families

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.030
over the holidays

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:13.300
and just a particular thanks also

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.050
to primary care colleagues.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:18.590
They delivered almost seven
million jabs in December,

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:19.980
and, of course, continued to work

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.883
to ensure routine services
are available as well.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:26.490
As we look ahead, the
indications do suggest

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.120
that the Omicron wave
is beginning to subside,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.720
although significant challenges
still remain, of course,

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.570
beyond just the numbers in hospital.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:36.540
As Steve said this morning

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.940
in response to the latest
weekly performance data,

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.430
pressure on urgent
emergency care pathways,

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.270
on ambulances, flow through hospitals

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:44.770
is still very high.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:46.800
Of course, we do need to continue to build

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.150
on the work that we did on discharge

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.290
but also the early work

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:53.230
of the Health and Social
Care Discharge Task Force

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.270
and on the great work that
Pauline has been doing

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:57.080
with regional teams, in particular,

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.280
to try and make sure people
can get the right help

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.810
in the right place at the right time.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.610
Maximising staffing capacity
is going to be crucial to this.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:07.690
Thankfully, COVID absences are falling

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.010
from the very high levels seen

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:10.850
in the two weeks following Christmas,

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:13.310
but they are still causing
concerns and issues

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:14.320
for many services,

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:15.960
so we do continue to work,

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:20.730
particularly with 111 and
999 services, on recruitment.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.010
Alongside that,

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:25.120
we're supporting NHS
organisations in implementing

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:30.030
the government's COVID
vaccine mandation policy,

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.660
called VCOD for short,

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:34.710
Vaccination as a Condition of Deployment.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:38.820
Vaccination among NHS staff,
in general, is very high,

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:39.880
with over 91%

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.720
of those with an ESR,
electronic staff record,

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.530
having had at least two doses recorded

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.850
and a higher proportion
of staff double jabbed

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.410
than the rest of the population.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:53.030
Numbers of unvaccinated
staff are reducing every day

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:55.760
due to the incredibly intensive work

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.390
that's happening across the NHS

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.390
to support people to get the vaccine,

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.700
but there will be geographical
and clinical areas

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.480
where we will need to support
organisations and systems

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.210
to mitigate the likely
impact of implementing VCOD

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:13.683
on service continuity
and on recovery plans.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:15.760
On recovery, as I've said,

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.120
we've seen some really great work

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.150
to keep elective and cancer
services going over this winter

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:21.100
thanks to a combination

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.410
of continued prioritisation,
protection, and separation

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.100
of elective care pathways,

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:27.460
use of the independent sector,

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:30.080
and benefited, of course,
from relatively low numbers

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.850
of COVID patients
requiring intensive care.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:36.310
However, elective
activity is obviously not

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:38.810
the only area impacted by the pandemic.

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:41.530
We do recognise the importance
of the focus on recovery

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:43.460
across all our services,

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.510
including mental health
and learning disabilities,

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.700
community services, and primary care.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.480
On elective, though,
considerable work remains

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.070
to increase activity levels

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:55.860
and treat some of those who've
been waiting the longest.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.240
We've discussed this before,

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.250
but it will be a multi-year challenge

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:00.900
and a range of ambitions and actions

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:03.910
have been identified and
communicated to the NHS

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:07.030
through the NHS Operational
Priorities and Planning Guidance

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:10.490
for 22/23 that went out
just before Christmas.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:13.730
Supporting that is 2.3 billion of revenue,

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:16.130
1.5 billion of extra capital funding

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:18.880
for new beds, equipment,
and surgical hubs,

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:20.850
and diagnostic services will be bolstered

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:24.300
by 410 million revenue
to continue the roll out

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.550
of community diagnostic
centres and upgrades

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:30.390
to endoscopy, breast
screening, and CT capacity,

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:33.870
but it will be necessary, despite
all of that, to take steps

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:38.870
both now and over the coming
quarters / the coming years

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:43.350
to achieve maximum possible
productivity in the context

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.840
of the continuing challenges
posed by the pandemic,

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:49.290
no easy task.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:52.350
As I said, it's not just
elective care we're focusing on.

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:54.560
The planning guidance sets
out recovery ambitions

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:56.400
for primary care,
community, mental health,

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.100
and learning disability,
and cancer services, too,

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:00.910
as well as, importantly, focusing

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:04.060
on the key enabling
factors like workforce,

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.930
better use of digital and
data, and integration.

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:10.140
We continue to see integrated care systems

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:11.330
and the structures within them

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.350
as the primary points of delivery

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:14.550
on all of this

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:15.860
into the future.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:18.280
As we all know, delays in
the parliamentary process

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:19.810
have met that our planning assumptions

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.310
for the shift in statutory
responsibilities,

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:23.377
the CCGs to ICB,

00:10:23.377 --> 00:10:25.570
has been put back to July,

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:27.860
but work continues to
support local systems

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:30.620
to be ready and to plan effectively

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:31.793
for the year ahead.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:33.670
At the last meeting,

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:36.760
we discussed how we mirror
that integration nationally

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:38.960
to streamline the support
provided to systems

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:41.399
and deliver improvements in care.

00:10:41.399 --> 00:10:43.170
I'd just like to thank Mark

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:45.990
for the work he's been leading,
working with colleagues

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.020
from Health Education
England, NHS Digital, and NHSX

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:53.380
towards creating a new
single organisation.

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:56.920
We are still in very
early days of that process

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:59.500
and indeed relatively
early in our thinking.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.470
But already obvious that
central to our discussions

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:04.820
is how we come together as
an effective organisation

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:07.540
with the right vision, values, and culture

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:08.540
to deliver the very best

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:11.080
for our patients, communities, and people.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:12.890
I think there is already, certainly

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:17.060
a real sense of enthusiasm
around achieving that goal.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:18.673
Final thing from me, David,

00:11:18.673 --> 00:11:19.970
I just want to acknowledge,

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:21.750
'cause I think it's important

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:23.530
that we acknowledge the experience

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.810
of some of our colleagues,
particularly women,

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:27.110
which were brought to light

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:29.870
in "The Times" last
week and on social media

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.350
and through a range of other
channels, too, actually,

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.980
clearly, the physical and mental safety

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:38.170
of every single member of NHS staff

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.400
is a fundamental expectation.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.630
Sexual harassment, assault,

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:44.850
physical violence, abuse, or bullying

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:47.220
should never be tolerated,

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:49.230
whoever the offender is.

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.610
Look, it's not a unique issue
to the NHS, by any means,

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:55.303
but it does not mean
it should be tolerated.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:58.930
I did raise this last week, Steve and I,

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:00.890
with leaders from the Royal Colleges,

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:02.570
'cause it was an issue

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:03.910
that been highlighted particularly

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.090
around some of our
professional colleagues.

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:07.560
We had a really positive discussion.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:09.660
There was an enthusiasm to work together,

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:11.600
including with our People Directorate,

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.650
to better understand
and tackle this problem.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:16.610
I would just like to
say to anyone listening

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:19.920
who's experienced this and
maybe hasn't come forward,

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:24.810
and that, to be clear, is
bullying, harassment, assault,

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.490
abuse for any reason,

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:29.774
anyone who has experienced it

00:12:29.774 --> 00:12:32.650
and anyone who has perhaps
not felt confident enough

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:34.240
to come forward,

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:37.330
not felt able to raise their concerns,

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:38.810
please do speak up.

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:40.830
We take this really seriously.

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:42.840
We want to root it out.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:46.923
We can really only do that
when we know about it. Steve.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:53.790
- Thanks, Amanda. Sorry,
just coming off mute.

00:12:55.030 --> 00:12:56.470
As Amanda has said,

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:59.160
the whole system throughout the NHS

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.660
has achieved such a huge amount

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:03.120
in the short space of time

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.220
since Omicron appeared,

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:07.500
towards the end of last year,

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:09.420
but it's also the case that once again,

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.080
we've had to ask lots of colleagues

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:12.820
throughout the health service

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:14.400
to do things in a different way

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.120
and sometimes to do things
that they don't usually do

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:19.930
and to focus on particular priorities.

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:22.650
As we come out of this particular wave

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:26.490
and Omicron infection rates start to fall,

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:27.860
we'll need to begin the process

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:30.950
of reversing that reallocation
and reprioritization

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:34.620
of staff time in both
primary and secondary care

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:37.860
and change from the priorities

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.210
that have been important and necessary

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:41.810
to respond to Omicron

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:44.220
in delivering the booster
campaign, for instance,

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.680
and responding to the large number

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:49.990
of patients that we've
once again had to manage

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:51.783
in our hospital systems.

00:13:52.770 --> 00:13:55.470
As I've mentioned, we are
now, for example, moving

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:58.360
to the next phase of the
vaccination programme,

00:13:58.360 --> 00:13:59.680
particularly for adults.

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:02.300
We've sometimes described
this as the hard yards,

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:04.490
where we're redoubling our efforts

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:06.090
on the outreach and engagement

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:10.140
to those who haven't had
the next dose of vaccine,

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:12.450
including their first
dose of the COVID vaccine,

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:15.220
so that evergreen offer is still there.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:18.300
We are still working encouraging
and persuading people

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:19.850
to come forward for the first dose,

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:21.250
the second dose, the booster dose,

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:23.420
and, in some cases, a fourth dose,

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:24.680
but the truth is

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:26.840
it's not going to be
quite at the high volume

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:29.050
that we saw towards the end of last year

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:31.490
in the next couple of months,

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:33.070
for obvious reasons.

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:34.310
That means that we are

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:36.300
in a position where we can stand down some

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:38.250
of that vaccination capacity,

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.130
particularly that that's been provided

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:43.500
by our primary care
networks and workforce,

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:46.570
and begin to allow our general
practitioners and their teams

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:50.650
to return to focus on
their usual activities,

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:52.760
for instance, patients
with long-term conditions

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:54.390
so that they can continue

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:57.240
to get the non-urgent
but important checkups

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:00.610
that are so important at
managing their conditions

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:02.890
but also avoiding potential need

00:15:02.890 --> 00:15:05.280
for acute care further down the line.

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.750
Today we have written out
to the system once again

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:10.290
to inform colleagues of this intention.

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:11.760
We would expect to see that shift,

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:13.220
particularly in primary care,

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:16.280
over the forthcoming days and weeks.

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:17.400
We do, of course, need to bear in mind

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:18.530
that this needs to be done in a way

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:20.910
that would allow services
to be stood back up

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:22.020
with minimum friction

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:24.750
in the event of a future need

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.720
to ramp up the vaccination
programme again,

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:28.070
but nevertheless, this is the point

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:31.730
where we can move some of that work

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:34.850
back into usual care.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:37.570
Now, of course, part of
this, also, is contingent

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:40.110
upon the work being done
with government to establish

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:43.370
what a sustainable future
delivery model looks like

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.820
for the COVID vaccine programme.

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:48.930
Of course, when government and JCVI decide

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:52.880
what the future need is going to be,

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:54.530
we will work with them.

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:57.780
Of course, the sooner we're
able to plan for that,

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:00.720
the better we can do so.

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.940
Same is also true for hospitals, in time,

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:05.330
although we can't forget

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:09.140
that we still have, as Amanda
said, very high numbers

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:12.610
of patients are in a hospital with COVID,

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:15.010
almost 14,000 patients,

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:17.310
as we meet here today,

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.220
with confirmed COVID.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:20.600
As, again, Amanda has alluded to,

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:23.680
the weekly statistics that
we put out this morning

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:25.410
showed that the last week was the busiest

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:27.100
for ambulance conveyances to A and E

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:28.703
since early December.

00:16:29.687 --> 00:16:33.500
Indeed, those pressures
throughout the NHS remain.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:35.660
In the Northwest, for example,

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:37.840
discussions are taking place today

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:39.480
around a potential activation

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.660
of the Nightingale surge hub

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.100
at Preston at the Royal Preston Hospital

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:47.410
to help with safe discharge and flow

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:50.530
for patients in the area
around that hospital.

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:54.320
Again, I think that really
reinforces the continuing impact

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:56.770
that COVID is having on the health service

00:16:56.770 --> 00:17:00.883
and the pressures that the
entire system remains under.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:06.610
As ever, we will be focusing
on balancing our priorities

00:17:06.610 --> 00:17:09.990
in terms of what we need
to provide for patients

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:11.940
as we come out of this wave,

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:13.300
balancing those priorities

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:16.320
in the way that clinicians
do, frankly, every day

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:18.060
in their usual practise

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:20.310
so that we can continue to focus

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:22.040
on urgent and emergency care needs

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.900
of both patients with COVID
and, of course, those without

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:28.250
and, increasingly, that we can ensure

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:30.870
that we are putting a
real focus and weight

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:34.810
behind the work on recovery
of elective services.

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:38.590
Once again, as we begin to
come out of the winter months.

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:40.000
I think, Amanda,

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.683
Ruth wanted to say a
few words as well; Ruth.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:46.060
- Thank you, Amanda, and thank you, Steve.

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:50.030
First of all, I'd like to
say a very big thank you.

00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:53.590
Thank you to those people that
are working extremely hard

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:56.590
to make vaccines available for our staff

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:58.500
and for the population,

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:00.400
those people working in the NHS,

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:03.803
those people that are retired
and come back to help us,

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:06.920
those people that are student nurses,

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.470
and, of course, our volunteers

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:12.030
who've made a huge contribution

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:13.763
to the vaccination efforts.

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:17.200
The tremendous amount
of work that is going on

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:19.350
to providing information and support

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:23.000
to those that may be hesitant
about having a vaccine

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.760
is hugely important.

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:30.390
I know those are really
important conversations.

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:32.280
It's because of that work

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.760
that we are seeing
COVID-vaccine-uptake rates,

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:36.280
as Amanda has said,

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:40.543
increasing steadily over
each day and each week.

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:46.320
COVID vaccination for uptake
in staff is important,

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:47.390
really important.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:50.090
It's important for
individual staff members.

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:52.720
It's important for patients' safety.

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:56.270
Lastly, it's important
for public confidence.

00:18:56.270 --> 00:18:57.440
Because we know

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:00.370
how much the trust the public has

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:02.403
in our health care professionals,

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:07.330
it's right that we now really concentrate

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:09.810
on supporting our staff

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:11.533
to take up the vaccination,

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:15.690
but leaders on the ground
are telling us also,

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:17.540
and Amanda and I met some yesterday

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:20.230
as part of my CNO strategic group.

00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:24.750
Then I went on to meet my
CNO ethnic diversity group.

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:28.000
They tell me some of those stories.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:29.180
They tell me, too,

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:32.690
about how compassionate
leadership right now

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:35.730
is what they're doing
to support their staff

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:37.853
during these very difficult decisions.

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:42.480
Over the last few weeks
and months, we've seen,

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.400
being in a level-four incident,

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:46.740
real pressure on staff.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:48.603
Those leaders also tell me,

00:19:50.607 --> 00:19:53.870
"We don't know where we'll end up by April

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:57.190
with the numbers of vaccine, staff,

00:19:57.190 --> 00:20:01.260
but actually, we know that
all of our staff are valued.

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:04.710
We really want to support
them to have their vaccination

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:07.730
'cause we want them into the future."

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:09.340
Thank you, Amanda.

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:10.290
- Ruth, Thank you.

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:12.470
I'd add, if I may for one second,

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:14.710
I think Ruth is absolutely right.

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:17.590
The government's objective of
universal uptake, of course,

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:19.110
is the right one.

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:21.700
Every colleague who takes
up their job is a positive

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:23.670
because that means they've
got additional protection

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:25.110
from the virus,

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:27.070
but at the same time,
there is a real element

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:28.290
of risk in this,

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:29.930
that local teams are facing.

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:31.980
There's a risk for our
national-level targets,

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:34.490
including the expansion of the workforce,

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:36.270
particularly in nursing.

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:37.730
Look, just to reassure the board,

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:39.140
I am keeping the Secretary of State

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:42.420
and his ministerial team
informed and up to date

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:43.633
on all of these issues.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:48.930
- Amanda, Steve, and Ruth,
thank you very much for that.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:50.610
It's very reassuring.

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:53.690
I wonder, Ara, if I could
throw you a slightly curve ball

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:54.573
on this one.

00:20:56.306 --> 00:21:00.120
You've been a clinician for some 40 years.

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:04.090
How would you place the
development of mRNA vaccines

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:07.370
in terms of the science
breakthroughs over that time?

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:10.380
Would they be one of the
major advances you've seen

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.520
in your time, would you think?

00:21:12.520 --> 00:21:14.970
- Absolutely, David,
it's transformational.

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:16.500
What's of interest is...

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:18.730
Something which is very
relevant to the NHS

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:22.040
is what makes people
take up an innovation.

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:23.960
In actual fact, mRNA vaccines,

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:26.360
well, mRNA technology, or the platform,

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:29.083
has been available for more
than a decade and a half.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:31.790
The challenge has been

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:35.110
how do you translate that
into clinical benefit?

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:37.630
I think the pandemic provided...

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:38.820
If there's one silver lining,

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:40.260
I don't think you can find much,

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:44.920
but it's the opportunity to
look at platform technologies,

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:49.713
like mRNA, which is essentially
a cargo-delivery mechanism.

00:21:50.896 --> 00:21:53.340
I think the second big transformation is

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:55.770
instead of putting a dead virus,

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:59.323
you can actually sequence this
on a silica on a computer.

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:02.300
I think if I'm correct,

00:22:02.300 --> 00:22:06.680
by the time the sequencing of
that virus arrived to a lab,

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:10.540
in 40 days, the first jab was injected

00:22:11.540 --> 00:22:13.860
into safety trials initially.

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:16.163
That is unheard of.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:18.440
I think we've all benefited

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:22.140
from this amazing, amazing innovation.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:23.860
I think in many ways

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:27.540
the NHS is the best
health system in the world

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:30.113
that could translate this fairly quickly.

00:22:31.384 --> 00:22:35.150
Thanks to the scientists
and many of the clinicians

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:36.910
in the health service,

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:39.880
we not only managed to
get it into the arms...

00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:42.940
Well, we got the first
patient ever in the world

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:44.410
vaccinated in this country,

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:47.120
but we managed to
expedite the vaccinations

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:48.643
at an unbelievable speed.

00:22:49.912 --> 00:22:52.250
Nice to see the back of it, I hope, soon.

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:56.020
- Do you that vaccines,
or equivalent of vaccines,

00:22:56.020 --> 00:22:59.100
are going to play a
bigger role in health care

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:02.690
as we go forward in terms of
the prevention of illness?

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:05.040
- Correct, the mRNA vaccine,

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:09.710
the initial hypothesis

00:23:09.710 --> 00:23:10.810
of the creativity

00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:13.793
was actually based on giving
someone a cancer vaccine,

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:16.810
which we've seen again at this country.

00:23:16.810 --> 00:23:20.000
We do cervical cancer
vaccines now very effectively

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:20.833
in this country.

00:23:20.833 --> 00:23:22.800
Thus hopefully, in the next decade or so,

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:26.240
we see a complete reduction
in cervical cancer,

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:30.040
so the answer to your question is, yes,

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:34.210
vaccines will play all
sorts of different roles

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:35.780
in preventing disease

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:37.930
or at least reducing the sensitivity

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:41.030
to whatever that foreign agent might be.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:43.040
It could be a pathogen like a virus.

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:46.120
It could be an allergen,
which is something

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:49.333
which is also challenging
us in the NHS, allergies.

00:23:51.050 --> 00:23:52.310
- Thanks very much, Ara.

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:54.290
That's really interesting.

00:23:54.290 --> 00:23:55.890
Tim, I think the other...

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:59.270
There are no silver
linings in this, really,

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:00.760
but the other big change

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:02.730
that's happened over this time,
of course, has been the use

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:04.393
of more digital technology.

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:07.510
Tim, you've now seen

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:09.630
what's happening in the
U.S. and what's over here.

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:10.890
What's your big takeout

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:15.940
of how digital has contributed
to the recovery over here?

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:22.670
- Well, I think, David,
we've seen real opportunities

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:26.023
taken advantage of because of the crisis.

00:24:27.289 --> 00:24:30.720
I'll just say on the supply chain side,

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:34.280
delivering the urgent resources,

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:36.870
our understanding of that supply chain,

00:24:36.870 --> 00:24:39.280
because of digital resources,

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:43.810
and where exactly therapeutics

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:45.500
like the vaccine

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:46.970
are being delivered,

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:49.700
that allows us in real time to understand

00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:52.733
whether or not we're getting
to all the people we need to.

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:57.433
When we find we're not,
we're able to rapidly pivot.

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:02.840
That is how the entire health
system should be operating.

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:04.570
It's how it's operated

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:09.130
with the vaccine distribution programme.

00:25:09.130 --> 00:25:10.690
We have a big opportunity now

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:15.340
and a big task in front of us

00:25:15.340 --> 00:25:20.340
now that we all have seen
that way of operating

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:24.380
that depends on frontline digitization.

00:25:24.380 --> 00:25:27.160
It depends on data,

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:31.050
secure, safe trusted infrastructure

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:33.320
for data interoperability.

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:38.320
It depends on our ability to
analyse that data very rapidly.

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:43.810
It depends on a very low
burden of data submission.

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:47.250
Actually, preferably, it
should all just be automated.

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:50.393
I think we've seen now
what could be delivered.

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:54.860
Now it is the goal of the
transformation director

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:56.340
with all my colleagues here

00:25:57.659 --> 00:25:59.673
to deliver that for the entire service.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:04.020
- Amanda, look, a huge thank
you from the board, really,

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:05.020
to you and your team.

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:06.630
I know you've been working 24-7,

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:10.630
well, till well before Christmas.

00:26:10.630 --> 00:26:14.110
So thank you and Steve, Ruth,

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:18.510
Tim, everybody else, David
for what you've done.

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:21.933
You've worked tirelessly, so
thank you for what you've done.

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:24.983
Shall we move on to the operations, then?

00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:29.220
David, David Sloman, would
you like to pick that up?

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:31.260
- Yes, very happy to do so.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:32.093
Thanks for that.

00:26:32.093 --> 00:26:33.410
Amanda and Steve and Ruth, I think,

00:26:33.410 --> 00:26:35.470
have set the context very well

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:37.113
for the performance report here.

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:39.640
As Amanda reminded us,

00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:41.700
we did call a level-four national incident

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:42.970
on the 13th of December.

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:45.190
Although there are many
reasons to be optimistic

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:48.210
that we are working through
this wave, the Omicron wave,

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:50.410
it's worth remember that
we are still in level four

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:54.120
and there are still 14,000
patients with COVID in NHS beds,

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:56.103
so we're still working our way through.

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:58.450
If I was to say that the two things

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:00.930
which would characterise the Omicron wave

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:02.230
compared to previous waves

00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:03.840
is there has been less demand

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:05.440
on our critical care services

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:06.740
across the NHS,

00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:08.530
which in many ways is a consequence

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:10.300
of the success of the vaccine programme

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:12.960
that we've just touched on earlier.

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:15.020
The other side of that coin
is because of the nature

00:27:15.020 --> 00:27:16.730
of the transmissibility of the virus,

00:27:16.730 --> 00:27:20.210
the biggest hit we have had
is staff sickness and absence

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:21.170
as a consequence of Omicron.

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:25.020
That's the thing that we've
grappled with the most.

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:27.490
Now, my report here covers

00:27:27.490 --> 00:27:29.120
all areas of NHS activity:

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.090
elective care, urgent/emergency care,

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:32.860
diagnostics, cancer, primary care,

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:34.670
discharge, mental health,
learning disabilities,

00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:37.250
screening, and vaccination/immunization.

00:27:37.250 --> 00:27:39.340
I don't intend going through
each and every one of them,

00:27:39.340 --> 00:27:40.173
you'll be pleased to know,

00:27:40.173 --> 00:27:42.690
'cause it's there to be read and digested,

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:44.990
but one of the reasons I list those things

00:27:44.990 --> 00:27:47.460
is, again, building on
comments of colleagues earlier

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:48.880
to remind ourselves of the breadth

00:27:48.880 --> 00:27:50.200
of the services that we provide

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:53.830
and to thank all staff working
in all of those service areas

00:27:53.830 --> 00:27:57.360
for their efforts over the
weeks and months gone by.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:01.360
I will just highlight three
or four areas, however.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:04.520
Personally, on elective care,
as all colleagues are aware,

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:07.920
we were making very good
progress before Omicron turned up

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.590
in dealing with the elective backlog.

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:12.930
We have had a bit of a pause on that.

00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:15.340
We did not need to stand
down all elective work,

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:17.500
although we did have to pause some,

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:20.000
but nonetheless, that does
still leave us in a position

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:23.400
where we have 304,000
people waiting over 52 weeks

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:24.233
for their care

00:28:24.233 --> 00:28:28.530
and 21,000 people waiting
over 104 weeks for their care.

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:31.720
We now do need to move into
that elective recovery programme

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.420
as we then move out of Omicron.

00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:35.600
Some of the data points

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:39.210
in the sections on the urgent care pathway

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:41.570
bear witness to Steve
Powis's comments earlier

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:43.180
about the pressure there's been

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:44.967
across the urgent care system.

00:28:44.967 --> 00:28:47.077
You also note in the
section in mental health

00:28:47.077 --> 00:28:50.260
that's across the physical
and mental health elements

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:52.080
of the urgent care pathway.

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:54.350
You can see that data about the increases

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:57.110
in the referrals to the
mental health crisis service,

00:28:57.110 --> 00:28:59.453
which is 30% up this year on last year.

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:03.570
Also, para nine draws out
the pressures that we face

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:05.483
within the ambulance service.

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:07.840
Two other things to highlight.

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:09.770
In the section on cancer,

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:12.650
we have been clear throughout the pandemic

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:15.467
that our cancer services are
there and are open for business

00:29:15.467 --> 00:29:16.530
and that anybody who thinks

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:19.050
they have any suspected cancer symptoms

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:21.130
should be presenting
themselves to general practice.

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:23.510
That message appears
to be getting through,

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:25.320
'cause, in November, you will note

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:28.890
that we saw 117% of
pre-COVID referral rates,

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:32.760
so those services are there
and are being utilised.

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:36.290
I think, finally, to touch on, once again,

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:39.113
the vaccination and
immunisation performance,

00:29:40.338 --> 00:29:42.913
you can see the data there in the pack.

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:48.470
So far we have delivered
over 112 million vaccines

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:49.560
during the course of this pandemic,

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:51.310
which is an extraordinary number.

00:29:51.310 --> 00:29:53.770
You can see the data on the
first and the follow-ups

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:54.873
and the boosters.

00:29:55.970 --> 00:29:57.730
I'm quite happy to take any questions

00:29:57.730 --> 00:30:00.370
on any of the sections I've
covered in the report, David,

00:30:00.370 --> 00:30:02.110
but if that's okay with you,

00:30:02.110 --> 00:30:03.563
I'll pass it over to Peter Ridley now

00:30:03.563 --> 00:30:07.080
just to conclude on the
finance report before we-

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:09.650
- Yeah, just before that, Mark,

00:30:09.650 --> 00:30:12.082
did you have any comment
you wanted to make?

00:30:12.082 --> 00:30:13.423
- No, thank you, David.

00:30:14.274 --> 00:30:16.110
David Sloman's covered them, thank you.

00:30:16.110 --> 00:30:19.490
- Any questions for David and Mark

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:22.313
before we move on to
the finance with Peter?

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:27.130
Maybe, David, I could just...

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:27.980
And Mark, actually,

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:31.350
'cause you've both been
chief executives of trusts

00:30:31.350 --> 00:30:32.463
for many years.

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:37.350
If you look back over
your careers and look

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:40.240
at the pressure on the
emergency service at the moment,

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:43.530
how do they compare with how
things have been in winter

00:30:43.530 --> 00:30:45.860
over the last 10-15 years

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:47.083
in your experience?

00:30:48.672 --> 00:30:51.080
- Shall I come in first, Mark?

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:52.160
One of the things I think

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:53.778
it's worth bearing in mind, though,

00:30:53.778 --> 00:30:57.643
is we are now in our third COVID winter.

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:01.630
When I use those words,
"third COVID winter,"

00:31:01.630 --> 00:31:03.920
I think that gives us some sense

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:07.080
of how hard this has been.

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:09.250
It's the consistency

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:11.440
that I think has had the biggest impact

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:14.620
on patients and staff.

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:18.030
Also, I think the three
waves gone through,

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:18.870
I think, proves it.

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:20.270
Running hospitals, I would argue

00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:22.080
that would probably be the toughest time

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:24.870
of my career, looking back.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:26.000
Steve Powis and I,

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.780
we did the Ebola patients back in the day,

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:31.720
but that was a very intensive
and short period of time.

00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:33.680
It's the amount of time
this is run through

00:31:33.680 --> 00:31:35.893
where I think has been most telling.

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:39.580
- David, if I could just add.

00:31:39.580 --> 00:31:42.890
I completely agree with
David's reflections there.

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:44.860
We talk often about the peaks,

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:47.510
but there's a huge amount
of activity that goes on

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:50.000
when we're trying to
recover and restore services

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:52.680
as we have done at earlier
stages of the pandemic.

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:56.950
The period of time that
our staff have been trying

00:31:56.950 --> 00:31:59.880
to respond to the great
number of challenges

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:01.140
that have come their way,

00:32:01.140 --> 00:32:03.400
it has been extraordinary.

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:04.810
We're doing our best to support them.

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:06.610
I think the challenge for our leaders

00:32:06.610 --> 00:32:08.690
in all parts of the
country is to make sure

00:32:08.690 --> 00:32:11.020
that they're balancing a
whole range of priorities

00:32:11.020 --> 00:32:13.360
and importantly to support

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:15.750
all of our frontline teams as well.

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:18.290
- Thanks, Mark, and Andrew, Andrew Morris,

00:32:18.290 --> 00:32:20.020
you've been particularly
concerned, I know,

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:22.300
about that interface

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:25.230
between secondary care and social care.

00:32:25.230 --> 00:32:27.390
You've also been working within the NHS

00:32:27.390 --> 00:32:29.190
for a long, long time.

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:32.180
How do you see things at the moment?

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:35.810
- I think for all providers
life is really tough.

00:32:35.810 --> 00:32:38.620
You work on a 24-hour basis

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:40.293
in terms of managing

00:32:40.293 --> 00:32:43.503
a flow of work that's coming
in and your discharges,

00:32:44.550 --> 00:32:46.500
so the relationship with social care

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:48.770
is really, really important.

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:51.350
Trying to get patients out in a timely way

00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:55.543
is crucial to keeping your
hospital going, really.

00:32:57.060 --> 00:32:59.500
When gridlock occurs,
unfortunately, you end up

00:32:59.500 --> 00:33:02.783
with delays and ambulances
queuing outside of A and E.

00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:06.300
Collectively with social care,

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:08.170
we have to improve our flow

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:10.260
through our various systems

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:12.000
that both social care manage

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:13.920
and we do on the health side.

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:17.250
That's not to say that health is perfect.

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:21.143
It isn't, in terms of
the flow of patients out

00:33:22.270 --> 00:33:25.200
where there's genuine health care needs

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:28.310
that need to be fulfilled
in other settings.

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:32.380
But I think the relationship
is really important

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:35.020
with local authorities and social care.

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:37.600
It's something we've got
to foster for the future.

00:33:37.600 --> 00:33:39.450
The one pressure point

00:33:39.450 --> 00:33:42.070
that I'm concerned about, in particular,

00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:44.030
is mental health.

00:33:44.030 --> 00:33:48.180
I've seen in the paragraph 29

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:50.530
that there's been particular pressures.

00:33:50.530 --> 00:33:54.620
I just want David's reflections perhaps

00:33:54.620 --> 00:33:57.840
on what we're doing to
increase the capacity

00:33:57.840 --> 00:34:00.310
to match the demand for it,

00:34:00.310 --> 00:34:03.120
'cause it's outstripping what
was originally envisaged,

00:34:03.120 --> 00:34:04.413
I think, for this winter.

00:34:07.780 --> 00:34:09.127
- Happy to reflect back, Andrew.

00:34:09.127 --> 00:34:10.470
You're absolutely right.

00:34:10.470 --> 00:34:12.243
As I did, I referenced
earlier the referrals

00:34:12.243 --> 00:34:14.260
into the mental health crisis services,

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:17.050
which are materially up year on year.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:19.360
As you're aware, Claire Murdoch
is our national director

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:20.420
of mental health services.

00:34:20.420 --> 00:34:23.210
She is working with
colleagues to make sure

00:34:23.210 --> 00:34:26.090
as we move into the months
and, indeed, the years ahead

00:34:26.090 --> 00:34:28.280
we do have capacity which is aligned

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:30.750
both to current and increasing demand,

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:32.840
which I think is consistent
across all elements

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:33.763
of the service.

00:34:35.380 --> 00:34:37.070
- Yeah, thank you.

00:34:37.070 --> 00:34:40.660
- Thanks, and maybe
lastly on this section,

00:34:40.660 --> 00:34:43.530
David Behan, you've been
involved in social care

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:45.190
for many, many years.

00:34:45.190 --> 00:34:47.080
How do you see this intersection

00:34:47.080 --> 00:34:48.770
or the important of the intersection

00:34:48.770 --> 00:34:51.420
between health care and social care?

00:34:51.420 --> 00:34:52.520
- Yeah, thanks, David.

00:34:54.840 --> 00:34:57.010
David's point about "this is not just

00:34:57.010 --> 00:34:58.890
a one-year phenomenon.

00:34:58.890 --> 00:35:01.710
It's a three-year phenomenon and ongoing"

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:05.120
has been the reality
in social care as well.

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:09.320
Arguably, the fragility in social care

00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:10.740
is more acute

00:35:11.950 --> 00:35:16.470
Therefore, the pressure that
has been built is significant,

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:17.970
but I think it's important, David,

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:19.520
to look to the future as well,

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:21.940
as you said at the top of this meeting.

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:23.880
I think there are opportunities

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:28.570
from the learning that has
taken place during COVID

00:35:28.570 --> 00:35:31.490
about where health and local
government more generally

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:34.270
but health and social
care have collaborated.

00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:35.150
Then I think

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:37.830
communities and people that
live in those communities

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:40.370
have had improved
services as a consequence.

00:35:40.370 --> 00:35:43.410
I think the opportunity that there is

00:35:43.410 --> 00:35:46.550
through the Health and Social
Care Bill and the creation

00:35:46.550 --> 00:35:49.240
of integrated care systems
and integrated care boards,

00:35:49.240 --> 00:35:50.653
looking to the future,

00:35:51.870 --> 00:35:54.910
is one that we must take with
both hands, it strikes me,

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:58.880
because that potentially can
change the nature of the game

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:00.163
as we go forward.

00:36:01.020 --> 00:36:04.410
As we begin to welcome improved figures

00:36:04.410 --> 00:36:06.940
in terms of infection rates,

00:36:06.940 --> 00:36:08.453
not everywhere but in,

00:36:10.450 --> 00:36:11.700
pardon me,

00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:13.020
lots of the country.

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:17.550
Then, I think, looking
forward to not just continuing

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:20.070
to comment on the
pressure that we're under

00:36:20.070 --> 00:36:22.700
but actually looking to
see what the opportunity is

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:24.100
to design a better future

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:27.483
is really important.

00:36:29.790 --> 00:36:33.873
Finally, I suppose, to
David Sloman's point,

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:36.830
I no longer chair HC-One,

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:39.640
but I described the couple

00:36:39.640 --> 00:36:41.290
of years that I did chair HC-One

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:43.460
as the most difficult
of my professional life

00:36:43.460 --> 00:36:45.003
by a long, long, long way.

00:36:46.540 --> 00:36:49.050
We shouldn't underestimate the challenges

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:51.600
of delivering services

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:54.870
during this pandemic.

00:36:54.870 --> 00:36:57.800
Wherever in the health and
care system we've operated,

00:36:57.800 --> 00:36:59.880
the challenges are real and significant,

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:01.003
but I do think

00:37:01.003 --> 00:37:02.970
that it's important as leaders

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:04.350
we look to a brighter future

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:06.570
and begin to describe what
that brighter future is.

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:09.530
I think Tim's point about transformation

00:37:09.530 --> 00:37:11.900
and to Amanda's point
about looking forward

00:37:11.900 --> 00:37:13.560
to the future around integrated care

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:15.350
is massively, massively important

00:37:15.350 --> 00:37:19.420
as we begin to come out of the pandemic.

00:37:19.420 --> 00:37:20.263
But thanks, David.

00:37:20.263 --> 00:37:21.770
- Thanks, David.

00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:24.360
Wol, then, Pat, Wol?

00:37:24.360 --> 00:37:26.883
- Yeah, just a quick one
because it's easy to focus

00:37:26.883 --> 00:37:29.340
when it comes to transformation/innovation

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:32.290
following the pandemic

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:33.603
which forced us to do certain things

00:37:33.603 --> 00:37:35.160
in a different way,

00:37:35.160 --> 00:37:37.010
but is it worth just bringing out

00:37:37.010 --> 00:37:39.310
some of the actual process innovation,

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:42.640
some of the ways we've been
working to keep patients safe

00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:45.440
but challenging ourselves
and the regulators

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:46.940
to rethink how we do things?

00:37:46.940 --> 00:37:50.129
I think that's just as important as well.

00:37:50.129 --> 00:37:51.870
Don't know whether Steve or
Amanda wants to talk about that,

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:52.770
or Ruth, actually.

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:55.840
- Yeah, I think that's a very good point.

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:59.560
Wol, out of adversity comes lots

00:37:59.560 --> 00:38:02.513
of changes that you would
not make in normal times.

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:05.350
Pat, did you want to come in?

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:09.590
Patrick?
- Thank you, David.

00:38:09.590 --> 00:38:10.723
Thank you, David, yes.

00:38:11.610 --> 00:38:14.200
It's been a remarkable
two years, hasn't it?

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:17.380
I think the sustained
performance over those two years

00:38:17.380 --> 00:38:18.380
has been incredible.

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:22.280
Thanks goes to everybody.

00:38:22.280 --> 00:38:25.040
Really, it's a question
for David Sloman, really.

00:38:25.040 --> 00:38:28.100
Obviously, the system
generally has coped very well.

00:38:28.100 --> 00:38:30.430
Some parts have coped better than others.

00:38:30.430 --> 00:38:34.070
Could you identify any
common characteristics

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:36.643
of the more successful responders?

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:41.463
- Okay, thanks for that, Patrick.

00:38:41.463 --> 00:38:43.770
One of the things I think...

00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:46.277
It's a bit like David
Prior just commented:

00:38:46.277 --> 00:38:49.330
"Out of adversity," et cetera.

00:38:49.330 --> 00:38:53.000
One of the consequences for
provider behaviour of Omicron

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:55.890
has been a much greater
willingness for organisations

00:38:55.890 --> 00:38:59.750
to collaborate and come together
and share infrastructure

00:38:59.750 --> 00:39:01.800
around common problems.

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:04.980
So I think the work, Patrick,
that we now move on to

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:06.700
in terms of how we make best use

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:08.130
of our provider collaboratives,

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:10.120
how we look at consolidate
and specialising some

00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:13.820
of our surgery, for example,
with the surgical hubs...

00:39:13.820 --> 00:39:16.720
I think it has been a much
more collaborative approach,

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:18.670
which has yielded the biggest dividends

00:39:19.741 --> 00:39:20.941
over the last few years.

00:39:22.970 --> 00:39:24.420
- I think, David, as well,

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:26.360
that collaborative approach applies

00:39:26.360 --> 00:39:28.050
across to the private sector as well;

00:39:28.050 --> 00:39:30.580
I know not everyone would
agree with me on this;

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:31.740
and, also, of course,

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:34.010
with the pharmaceutical
industry as well on vaccines

00:39:34.010 --> 00:39:36.530
that Ara was talking about earlier on.

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:38.420
We don't have all the answers ourselves.

00:39:38.420 --> 00:39:39.583
- Yeah, no, I agree.

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:44.240
- Steve Powis and then Amanda.

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:46.660
- Yes, well, I thought I should
respond to Wol's question

00:39:46.660 --> 00:39:48.520
before we passed it by.

00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:50.300
Amanda and Ruth might want to come in.

00:39:50.300 --> 00:39:51.750
I think the answer is there are lots

00:39:51.750 --> 00:39:53.150
of changes that have occurred,

00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:54.950
both large and small, during the pandemic

00:39:54.950 --> 00:39:57.570
that we need to capture and hold onto

00:39:57.570 --> 00:39:59.130
because they've benefited patients,

00:39:59.130 --> 00:40:01.770
have benefited public, benefited staff,

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:04.240
and, hopefully, all three.

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:06.270
The Beneficial Changes
Network that's being led

00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:08.660
by Hugh McCaughey and
Improvement Directorate

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:10.910
has been the probe that
has looked to capture those

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:15.910
and sought individual organisation
systems and individuals

00:40:16.034 --> 00:40:18.700
to tell us about those changes.

00:40:18.700 --> 00:40:21.480
But I think some of the bigger
ones that I would mention,

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:22.970
and this is not an exhaustive list

00:40:22.970 --> 00:40:24.240
and it's "in no particular order"

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:26.070
as they say on those quiz shows,

00:40:26.070 --> 00:40:28.880
are, I think, the
introduction of virtual wards.

00:40:28.880 --> 00:40:32.660
Again, we've seen programmes
in this area in the past,

00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:34.160
but I think the coordinated programme

00:40:34.160 --> 00:40:36.820
that Ian Dodge and the team have led

00:40:36.820 --> 00:40:38.560
with assistance from
primary care and others

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:40.180
is something that we really need to think

00:40:40.180 --> 00:40:42.780
about how we hold on to
that model in the future.

00:40:42.780 --> 00:40:43.680
Much more we can do

00:40:43.680 --> 00:40:46.290
around avoiding people coming to hospital

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:50.000
by providing that in-reach
of care into people's homes.

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:53.160
We talked about the vaccination
and the therapeutics

00:40:53.160 --> 00:40:57.430
programmme and getting
those out at pace and scale,

00:40:57.430 --> 00:41:02.140
but, of course, on drugs, in particular,

00:41:02.140 --> 00:41:04.200
determining what works in a clinical trial

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:05.033
is the first step.

00:41:05.033 --> 00:41:07.590
The second step is getting
it into clinical practise.

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:11.810
We know that there's been
a frustrating time lag

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:13.510
between those two things happening,

00:41:13.510 --> 00:41:17.060
so the work of RAPID C-19,

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:18.380
which has been the collaborative programme

00:41:18.380 --> 00:41:21.000
to get those clinical trial findings

00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:22.720
quickly into clinical practise

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:23.920
in a matter of days,

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:25.720
rather than months or years,

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:26.840
has been really fantastic.

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:29.700
We've learnt a lot about how we get things

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:31.393
into practise quickly.

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:35.140
Then, of course, staff
have worked flexibly

00:41:35.140 --> 00:41:38.820
across different systems

00:41:38.820 --> 00:41:40.990
and across different specialties.

00:41:40.990 --> 00:41:44.860
Retaining that generality of skills

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:46.680
rather than just specialist skills,

00:41:46.680 --> 00:41:49.300
again, is something we were
focused on before the pandemic,

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:51.630
but something that I
think we need to capture

00:41:51.630 --> 00:41:53.830
and do more of are post pandemic.

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:57.280
There's a huge list of things
that we're all reflecting on.

00:41:57.280 --> 00:41:58.800
Of course, the use of data,

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:01.670
the vaccine programme
has been a huge success

00:42:01.670 --> 00:42:04.580
not least because its ability
to capture real-time data

00:42:04.580 --> 00:42:08.440
and analyse what it needed
to do to improve day on day.

00:42:08.440 --> 00:42:10.470
Tim has talked about
that, I think, earlier.

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:12.700
I think these are all
things that we are taking

00:42:12.700 --> 00:42:13.880
out of the pandemic

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:17.440
and looking to embed in
our business as usual

00:42:17.440 --> 00:42:18.423
as we go forward.

00:42:19.920 --> 00:42:22.170
- Thanks, Steve. Amanda then Ruth?

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:23.230
- Thank you.

00:42:23.230 --> 00:42:25.870
I agree with absolutely
everything that Steve has said.

00:42:25.870 --> 00:42:27.890
I think one of the really important things

00:42:27.890 --> 00:42:29.480
that he's also said is just this point

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:31.010
about not forgetting all of that

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:33.470
but really thinking how do we
build on it as we go forward,

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:36.330
because it will be
fundamental to our recovery

00:42:36.330 --> 00:42:39.010
that we do do things differently,

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:40.730
that we don't go back to just doing things

00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:42.093
the same way we used to.

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:45.490
It is partly more, but
it's partly different,

00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:48.120
but, I suppose, two things for me to add,

00:42:48.120 --> 00:42:50.200
one, a note of caution

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:52.520
because Wol talked about processes

00:42:53.574 --> 00:42:55.910
as well as content things.

00:42:55.910 --> 00:42:57.510
I think there is just something for me

00:42:57.510 --> 00:43:01.040
about we only have so many staff,

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:04.050
so we were able to move incredibly quickly

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:04.920
during the pandemic

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:09.880
because we had, in a sense,
the obviousness of the mandate

00:43:09.880 --> 00:43:12.780
to focus on the urgent
priority in front of us

00:43:12.780 --> 00:43:13.750
at that moment.

00:43:13.750 --> 00:43:16.530
Vaccination's a great example.

00:43:16.530 --> 00:43:18.480
We had to stop doing stuff

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:20.350
in order to redirect resource

00:43:20.350 --> 00:43:22.120
to support vaccination.

00:43:22.120 --> 00:43:25.440
Similarly, if you think back
to the impact on elective,

00:43:25.440 --> 00:43:27.380
we have had to

00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:28.942
because we haven't had the workforce,

00:43:28.942 --> 00:43:30.170
we haven't had the beds,

00:43:30.170 --> 00:43:31.020
we haven't had

00:43:32.255 --> 00:43:34.230
the physical capacity
and theatres, et cetera,

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:36.860
to be able to maintain
all of that elective work

00:43:36.860 --> 00:43:40.460
whilst dealing with those
big surges in COVID...

00:43:40.460 --> 00:43:43.200
It's left a real backlog to tackle,

00:43:43.200 --> 00:43:45.990
so some of the things
that I think we were able

00:43:45.990 --> 00:43:48.270
to do at pace over COVID

00:43:48.270 --> 00:43:50.120
we cannot assume we can operate

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:52.360
at the same pace on everything

00:43:52.360 --> 00:43:53.840
on an ongoing basis

00:43:53.840 --> 00:43:55.960
unless we're prepared to say

00:43:55.960 --> 00:43:57.780
it's at the expense of something else.

00:43:57.780 --> 00:43:59.240
As we head into recovery, I think,

00:43:59.240 --> 00:44:01.650
that becomes a more and
more difficult thing to say,

00:44:01.650 --> 00:44:03.640
because the agenda for recovery

00:44:03.640 --> 00:44:06.400
is across so many of
those different areas.

00:44:06.400 --> 00:44:08.610
I think the caution for me is,
yes, we've learned something

00:44:08.610 --> 00:44:11.130
about how we can move really
quickly when we need to,

00:44:11.130 --> 00:44:13.500
but we shouldn't assume that
that is a model of working

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:16.970
that is either appropriate, frankly,

00:44:16.970 --> 00:44:21.040
or deliverable or desirable
in a recovery environment

00:44:21.040 --> 00:44:24.380
rather than a
pandemic-emergency-response environment.

00:44:24.380 --> 00:44:26.220
But second thing, on a more positive note,

00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:28.770
I think the things that
I would say on process

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:29.900
that has been critical

00:44:29.900 --> 00:44:32.010
has been the way that we
nationally have worked

00:44:32.010 --> 00:44:34.340
in partnership with other ALBs

00:44:34.340 --> 00:44:36.380
and with other key stakeholders.

00:44:36.380 --> 00:44:37.510
I think about the way

00:44:37.510 --> 00:44:39.010
that Steve, for example, meets

00:44:39.010 --> 00:44:42.090
with Royal College presidents
literally every week;

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:43.560
now it might be a fortnightly,

00:44:43.560 --> 00:44:45.810
but it, at one point, I
think, was almost every day;

00:44:45.810 --> 00:44:47.210
and the way that we've worked together

00:44:47.210 --> 00:44:48.680
across government as well.

00:44:48.680 --> 00:44:52.290
We're not, any of us,
thinking, I think, anymore

00:44:52.290 --> 00:44:54.010
that we would do anything in isolation.

00:44:54.010 --> 00:44:55.210
That partnership approach

00:44:55.210 --> 00:44:58.450
between the NHS national
team, regional teams,

00:44:58.450 --> 00:45:02.010
ICSs, local providers

00:45:02.010 --> 00:45:05.160
is the other thing that
I think has been critical

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:06.840
to the success of the last two years.

00:45:06.840 --> 00:45:11.130
The rhythm of engagement,
which again, sometimes has been

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:13.990
multiple webinars and sessions every week,

00:45:13.990 --> 00:45:17.410
I'm not suggesting we'd continue
to do anything like that,

00:45:17.410 --> 00:45:19.140
but how we just make sure

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:22.930
that we really do lean into that one NHS

00:45:22.930 --> 00:45:26.040
through all of our different layers

00:45:26.040 --> 00:45:29.620
and the one partnership
approach across social care

00:45:29.620 --> 00:45:33.100
but also across our other key
national partners and others,

00:45:33.100 --> 00:45:35.200
for me, is a characteristic

00:45:35.200 --> 00:45:37.410
of how we need to do things going forward

00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:39.730
as well as the critical process reflection

00:45:39.730 --> 00:45:42.030
on what's worked well
over the last two years.

00:45:43.060 --> 00:45:43.893
- Thanks, Amanda.

00:45:43.893 --> 00:45:46.010
Then lastly, Ruth.

00:45:46.010 --> 00:45:46.843
- Thanks very much.

00:45:46.843 --> 00:45:49.610
Going back to Wol's question,

00:45:49.610 --> 00:45:51.790
the one thing I'm very proud of

00:45:51.790 --> 00:45:53.580
during this pandemic response

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:57.470
is how we've cared for children
with respiratory viruses.

00:45:57.470 --> 00:46:00.250
Most winters, we see a huge peak

00:46:00.250 --> 00:46:04.800
in children needing critical
care, needing inpatients.

00:46:04.800 --> 00:46:07.110
This was a real bit of
work that we started

00:46:07.110 --> 00:46:08.820
back last May and June

00:46:08.820 --> 00:46:12.280
about "how do we take the
data from working with UKHSA?

00:46:12.280 --> 00:46:14.230
How do we make sure we take the learning

00:46:14.230 --> 00:46:17.810
from some of the community
oximetry at home?

00:46:17.810 --> 00:46:20.890
Work? Equally, some of the digital work?"

00:46:20.890 --> 00:46:23.360
Actually, we worked
across all four nations,

00:46:23.360 --> 00:46:28.290
so we came together as a UK
with clinical leadership.

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:31.420
I've got huge respect for
Duncan Burton, Simon Kenny

00:46:31.420 --> 00:46:34.710
that have worked together
to lead some of this work,

00:46:34.710 --> 00:46:36.730
but also, we've worked with volunteers,

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:38.280
with the charity sector,

00:46:38.280 --> 00:46:40.320
grateful too with HE partners

00:46:40.320 --> 00:46:43.140
with James developing
the training programme

00:46:43.140 --> 00:46:46.520
for paediatricians and paediatric nurses.

00:46:46.520 --> 00:46:48.490
Actually, it's that collaborative working

00:46:48.490 --> 00:46:51.020
that all of us coming together

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:56.020
has been working in partnership
using data but at pace.

00:46:56.250 --> 00:46:58.940
I think that's the type
of transformational work

00:46:58.940 --> 00:47:02.153
that we can do into the
future and indeed now.

00:47:02.153 --> 00:47:04.050
I was only talking to the team last week.

00:47:04.050 --> 00:47:06.637
We're planning now for next winter.

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:09.830
- Thanks very much, Ruth.

00:47:09.830 --> 00:47:12.500
Shall we move on then to the finances

00:47:12.500 --> 00:47:14.310
and then, after that,
the planning guidance?

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:15.373
That's you, Peter.

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:19.540
- Yeah, on the finances,
it's in the paper,

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:20.910
so I won't say an awful lot.

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:23.970
We're forecasting to come
in within core budget,

00:47:23.970 --> 00:47:26.030
so we've a surplus of 1.3 billion

00:47:26.030 --> 00:47:29.020
on the core NHS commissioning
and provider budget.

00:47:29.020 --> 00:47:31.423
That's against the plan of 154 billion.

00:47:32.360 --> 00:47:33.490
As well as efficiencies,

00:47:33.490 --> 00:47:35.440
we're seeing there is certainly the impact

00:47:35.440 --> 00:47:38.190
of operational pressures and Omicron,

00:47:38.190 --> 00:47:39.820
meaning that we haven't
been able to deliver all

00:47:39.820 --> 00:47:41.920
of the investments we would have liked to

00:47:41.920 --> 00:47:43.470
and have had to defer some programmes,

00:47:43.470 --> 00:47:47.180
so that is partly driving
the surplus we've got there.

00:47:47.180 --> 00:47:50.420
Finally, just in terms
of provider capital,

00:47:50.420 --> 00:47:52.260
we're forecasting a small underspend

00:47:52.260 --> 00:47:54.380
on the provider capital

00:47:54.380 --> 00:47:56.770
and are working through
that at the moment.

00:47:56.770 --> 00:47:58.620
We're on track on the finances,

00:47:58.620 --> 00:47:59.910
so there's probably not an awful lot more

00:47:59.910 --> 00:48:01.315
I'll say than that.

00:48:03.407 --> 00:48:07.100
- Did anyone have any questions
for Peter on the finances?

00:48:07.100 --> 00:48:08.760
Well, can we move on to
the planning guidance

00:48:08.760 --> 00:48:09.810
for next year, Peter?

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:12.570
- Yep, certainly.

00:48:12.570 --> 00:48:14.520
You've got the paper in the pack.

00:48:14.520 --> 00:48:16.270
It's there to note.

00:48:16.270 --> 00:48:18.520
Just to summarise what you've got,

00:48:18.520 --> 00:48:20.620
it's the operational planning guidance.

00:48:20.620 --> 00:48:22.320
The board's previously discussed

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:24.580
the NHS priorities and
planning for next year,

00:48:24.580 --> 00:48:27.430
so this is pulling together where we are.

00:48:27.430 --> 00:48:29.300
The main planning guidance,
as Amanda described,

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:31.970
was published on the 24th of December,

00:48:31.970 --> 00:48:35.190
so the process of planning
out in the NHS is underway.

00:48:35.190 --> 00:48:37.350
That will consist of draft plans

00:48:37.350 --> 00:48:38.820
by the middle of March

00:48:38.820 --> 00:48:41.160
and then final plans at the end of April.

00:48:41.160 --> 00:48:42.370
We are still working through some

00:48:42.370 --> 00:48:44.060
of the detailed technical guidance.

00:48:44.060 --> 00:48:45.450
That's being finalised with government,

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:47.910
so that'll be published in due course.

00:48:47.910 --> 00:48:49.420
The paper summarises

00:48:49.420 --> 00:48:52.900
how we're going about setting
the budgets and planning.

00:48:52.900 --> 00:48:54.510
It reflects the spending review settlement

00:48:54.510 --> 00:48:56.390
announced by government.

00:48:56.390 --> 00:48:59.100
You'll see the approach
is the one we've discussed

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:04.060
of moving back towards
businesses-as-normal framework

00:49:04.060 --> 00:49:06.110
in how we operate the financial regime

00:49:06.110 --> 00:49:08.820
and how we go about managing
the finance of the NHS.

00:49:09.980 --> 00:49:12.790
The shape of the resources
we've got described in here.

00:49:12.790 --> 00:49:14.430
Again, consistent with the spending review

00:49:14.430 --> 00:49:17.600
is an expectation of lower
levels of COVID demand

00:49:17.600 --> 00:49:19.580
so lower additional COVID costs,

00:49:19.580 --> 00:49:21.960
but with that tapering
over a number of years.

00:49:21.960 --> 00:49:24.180
Table one describes the tapering of those,

00:49:24.180 --> 00:49:27.280
those non-recurrent COVID
costs distorting somewhat

00:49:27.280 --> 00:49:28.390
the comparison between years,

00:49:28.390 --> 00:49:31.233
but that's one of the big swing factors.

00:49:32.370 --> 00:49:36.023
In line with how we'd go about
a normal financial framework,

00:49:38.179 --> 00:49:40.000
we've described how allocations work

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:43.353
and how fair-share target
allocations have been updated.

00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:45.650
As we would usually do,

00:49:45.650 --> 00:49:47.380
we've taken the recommendations

00:49:47.380 --> 00:49:49.030
of the independent Advisory Committee

00:49:49.030 --> 00:49:51.650
on Resource Allocations, ACRA,

00:49:51.650 --> 00:49:54.110
and built that into how
allocations will be made

00:49:54.110 --> 00:49:56.893
down to a commissioner and place level.

00:49:57.970 --> 00:50:00.760
Finally, the paper goes on to
describe some of the things

00:50:00.760 --> 00:50:02.170
you've already discussed in this meeting

00:50:02.170 --> 00:50:03.360
so continued commitments

00:50:03.360 --> 00:50:05.720
to mental health, primary
care, and other areas,

00:50:05.720 --> 00:50:08.370
as well as the deployment
of additional resources

00:50:08.370 --> 00:50:10.520
for elective recovery.

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:12.040
It's clear that there's an expectation

00:50:12.040 --> 00:50:14.160
that we'll increase the
delivery of efficiency.

00:50:14.160 --> 00:50:15.720
We will take out some of these elements

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:18.160
of COVID costs we've already described,

00:50:18.160 --> 00:50:19.990
we'll restore productivity levels,

00:50:19.990 --> 00:50:22.000
and we'll continue to
recover elective performance

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:24.493
with the dedicated resource
we've got to do that.

00:50:25.550 --> 00:50:27.217
That's probably the broad
summary of the paper.

00:50:27.217 --> 00:50:29.910
As I say, it's to note
here to bring together

00:50:29.910 --> 00:50:31.373
where we stand as of today.

00:50:33.510 --> 00:50:34.990
- Thanks, Peter, very much for that.

00:50:34.990 --> 00:50:38.350
Does anyone have any particular
questions for Peter there?

00:50:38.350 --> 00:50:41.400
One general question,
maybe the question's best

00:50:41.400 --> 00:50:44.687
for you, Amanda, or David or Mark,

00:50:44.687 --> 00:50:45.900
but I haven't actually walked

00:50:45.900 --> 00:50:49.240
round, for obvious reasons,
a hospital recently,

00:50:49.240 --> 00:50:50.730
because of infection-control issues,

00:50:50.730 --> 00:50:55.390
but if you take one of
our older hospitals,

00:50:55.390 --> 00:50:57.680
where there's very few single rooms,

00:50:57.680 --> 00:51:00.840
where entrances into
A and E are compressed

00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:02.903
and probably only one way into A and E,

00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:06.630
in terms of the impact on productivity

00:51:09.020 --> 00:51:12.730
in one of those older DGH hospitals,

00:51:12.730 --> 00:51:14.803
what is your general view about that?

00:51:16.060 --> 00:51:17.110
- I wonder, David,

00:51:17.110 --> 00:51:19.750
if it would be worth
bringing Pauline in on this,

00:51:19.750 --> 00:51:23.360
because Pauline's done an awful
lot of work with hospitals

00:51:23.360 --> 00:51:26.240
on exactly these sorts of constraints.

00:51:26.240 --> 00:51:27.810
Indeed, Ruth might want to comment as well

00:51:27.810 --> 00:51:30.010
in relation to infection control,

00:51:30.010 --> 00:51:32.490
but I think you're right
to raise it because...

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:34.620
I mentioned a moment ago
we've only got so many staff.

00:51:34.620 --> 00:51:37.430
Well, we've also only
got the estate we've got.

00:51:37.430 --> 00:51:39.810
That is a significant factor

00:51:39.810 --> 00:51:42.530
in what, of course, we're now able to do.

00:51:42.530 --> 00:51:44.530
But perhaps if Pauline wants to come in?

00:51:48.020 --> 00:51:50.910
- Yes, David, I think
Steve probably commented

00:51:50.910 --> 00:51:53.250
in one of our recent meetings

00:51:53.250 --> 00:51:57.230
that if we had an NHS estate
that had all single rooms,

00:51:57.230 --> 00:51:58.760
our ability to cope

00:51:58.760 --> 00:52:02.380
and to be able to
continue care during COVID

00:52:02.380 --> 00:52:06.180
would have been different to
what we've had experienced.

00:52:06.180 --> 00:52:08.993
Ruth might want to say a
little bit more about that.

00:52:09.880 --> 00:52:11.940
From that perspective,

00:52:11.940 --> 00:52:14.590
if we could imagine a situation tomorrow

00:52:14.590 --> 00:52:17.660
that the NHS was suddenly
single rooms throughout,

00:52:17.660 --> 00:52:21.210
then our ability to cope
with future COVID waves

00:52:21.210 --> 00:52:22.910
would be different.

00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:24.920
The flow through our
hospitals would be different.

00:52:24.920 --> 00:52:26.500
From a UEC point of view,

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:29.830
we'd be able to maintain a
lot of our elective work.

00:52:29.830 --> 00:52:32.940
So clearly have a big difference,

00:52:32.940 --> 00:52:35.740
but I think it's not just
things like single rooms.

00:52:35.740 --> 00:52:37.190
It's if we just look out

00:52:37.190 --> 00:52:40.280
right from the front door
of our hospitals through,

00:52:40.280 --> 00:52:42.440
so many of our emergency departments,

00:52:42.440 --> 00:52:44.910
and over the years, I think, I've been

00:52:44.910 --> 00:52:45.743
in all of them

00:52:45.743 --> 00:52:49.110
and in some of them on a regular basis,

00:52:49.110 --> 00:52:51.110
are really probably not very different

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:53.890
to what they were many, many years ago.

00:52:53.890 --> 00:52:56.440
Now, clearly, year in, year out,

00:52:56.440 --> 00:52:58.920
we are upgrading, et cetera,

00:52:58.920 --> 00:53:00.410
but we're trying to do what we can

00:53:00.410 --> 00:53:02.650
with the estate that we've got.

00:53:02.650 --> 00:53:06.380
Those emergency departments
that have got cubicles

00:53:06.380 --> 00:53:08.090
that are like glass cubicles;

00:53:08.090 --> 00:53:10.870
for example, you will see
them in Leicester, et cetera;

00:53:10.870 --> 00:53:13.820
again, from an IPC point
of view very different

00:53:13.820 --> 00:53:16.290
and how we can flow our patients through,

00:53:16.290 --> 00:53:19.850
but I think it really covers
just about all aspects

00:53:19.850 --> 00:53:22.050
of the NHS estate.

00:53:22.050 --> 00:53:24.610
I think out there, people
are doing their very best

00:53:24.610 --> 00:53:25.540
year on year

00:53:25.540 --> 00:53:28.980
to try and improve the
environment for our patients

00:53:28.980 --> 00:53:31.470
to give them better privacy
and dignity and everything,

00:53:31.470 --> 00:53:34.370
but it's also important from
a productivity point of view.

00:53:36.230 --> 00:53:38.250
- Can I comment, then, David?

00:53:38.250 --> 00:53:40.093
Yeah, absolutely, we know

00:53:40.093 --> 00:53:43.050
that where people have
had more single rooms,

00:53:43.050 --> 00:53:45.900
they've been able to cohort patients

00:53:45.900 --> 00:53:50.350
better, safer, and less
nosocomial transmission,

00:53:50.350 --> 00:53:52.813
but also, those people
have got bigger EDs.

00:53:53.670 --> 00:53:55.280
There's lots of organisations

00:53:55.280 --> 00:53:57.060
that have got emergency departments

00:53:57.060 --> 00:53:58.620
that were built for the number

00:53:58.620 --> 00:54:01.023
of patients going in them 10-15 years ago.

00:54:01.950 --> 00:54:04.240
Those people that are at bigger EDs,

00:54:04.240 --> 00:54:07.550
those people got more
space in between the beds

00:54:07.550 --> 00:54:09.200
and, of course, the ventilation.

00:54:09.200 --> 00:54:11.320
So, yeah, the new hospital programme

00:54:11.320 --> 00:54:15.090
working with Natalie Forrest
and colleagues there.

00:54:15.090 --> 00:54:19.150
But absolutely, single rooms
and investment in estates

00:54:19.150 --> 00:54:21.330
will be one of the big learning, for me,

00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:22.800
out of this pandemic

00:54:22.800 --> 00:54:25.640
with regard to
infection-prevention control.

00:54:25.640 --> 00:54:26.800
- I think of the old hospital

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:28.630
that used to be chief executive of, Ruth,

00:54:28.630 --> 00:54:30.711
if you remember, in King's Lynn.

00:54:30.711 --> 00:54:33.100
- Very lovely Queen Elizabeth Hospital,

00:54:33.100 --> 00:54:34.750
King's Lynn, absolutely.

00:54:34.750 --> 00:54:37.530
Great staff but needing that investment

00:54:37.530 --> 00:54:38.830
in the building, aren't they?

00:54:38.830 --> 00:54:40.510
- It was needing an investment
when you were there,

00:54:40.510 --> 00:54:41.460
and that was

00:54:41.460 --> 00:54:43.490
quite a few years ago.
- Indeed, some time ago.

00:54:43.490 --> 00:54:45.022
Don't age me, now, David, please.

00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:51.060
- Is there any questions for Peter

00:54:51.060 --> 00:54:52.820
that any would like to raise?

00:54:52.820 --> 00:54:54.623
Otherwise, any other business

00:54:54.623 --> 00:54:56.383
that anyone would like to raise?

00:54:57.240 --> 00:54:58.683
Or shall we call it a day?

00:54:59.630 --> 00:55:01.930
Good, well, look, Amanda,
thank you very much.

00:55:03.360 --> 00:55:05.270
For the next public meeting,

00:55:05.270 --> 00:55:06.960
we'll have a few other
issues on the agenda,

00:55:06.960 --> 00:55:10.910
but in the meantime thanks to
you and your team very much.

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:11.743
Goodbye.

